Q6: Is "extropy" the opposite of "entropy"?

Q6: Yes and no. As I see it, extropy is the metaphorical opposite of
entropy, but only metaphorically because while entropy is a
well-established concept (group of concepts, really) from physics and
information theory, extropy is more of a cultural concept.

Entropy, it is said, always increases. But this only a paraphrase of the
second law of thermodynamics. The crucial qualification on this law is
that entropy always increases in a *closed* system. It is our good
fortune that human beings are open systems, that the Earth is an open
system, that the solar system is an open system, and that our galaxy is
an open system. It has not even been positively established that our
universe is not an open system.

The evolution of life is a good example of increasing extropy, yet the
evolution of life required no contradiction of the second law of
thermodynamics. In the Extropian Principles 2.5, Max More suggests this
definition of extropy: 

"EXTROPY -- A measure of intelligence, information, energy, vitality,
experience, diversity, opportunity, and growth."

Not *exactly* the opposite of entropy, but the opposite of entropy makes
a good metaphor for what we mean by this idea.   [ewf]

